<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tomyris</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">6th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tomyris was a queen of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massagetae">Massagetae</a>, a nomadic Iranian people east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caspian-sea">Caspian Sea</a>, in the 6th century BCE. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> captured her son by a trick, she defeated the Persian army in 530 BCE, killed Cyrus and had his head put in a wineskin filled with blood.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tomyris.jpg" alt="Tomyris" width="320" height="508" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Andrea del Castagno · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tomyris-Castagno.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Herodotus relates that Cyrus the Great was killed in 530 BCE fighting the Massagetae, a people east of the Caspian whom many scholars place within the Saka world, under their queen <b>Tomyris</b>; the story is literary, but it probably reflects a real Persian defeat.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saka">Saka · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/kazakhstan">Kazakhstan</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/kazakhstan">Kazakhstan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.3,63.9&amp;year=-535" aria-label="Central Asia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6068 565 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Tomyris"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6518 835h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6518 835h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.3,63.9&amp;year=-535">What was here in 535 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…but if thou shalt not do so, I swear to thee by the Sun, who is lord of the Massagetai, that surely I will give thee thy fill of blood, insatiable as thou art.&#8221;</p><footer>Herodotus, Histories, book 1 (message to Cyrus, who had captured her son) &#183; tr. G. C. Macaulay, 1890 &#183; Herodotus' account &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2707/pg2707-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…cease to do so, I say, and be king over thine own people, and endure to see us ruling those whom we rule.&#8221;</p><footer>Herodotus, Histories, book 1 (message to Cyrus as he bridged the river into her land) &#183; tr. G. C. Macaulay, 1890 &#183; Herodotus' account &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2707/pg2707-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 539 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
